A||||3||JohnnyK||New clutch line, and new strange thing (problem?)||||1||
Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-10-2000||08:36 PM||||Well, I put the new clutch line in, and got everything sort of together (it's 3 custom made pieces, all jury-rigged together. I got a feeling the couplers are going to leak..) But anyways, my shifting is not quite good yet, but there is one really strange thing. You guys told me that a good way to bleed the slave cylinder is to take the bolt out, pull the plunger in, and then screw the bolt back in. However, when I used to do this, the master cylinder would basically run bone dry. Now, it barely takes half an inch out of it... Maybe less than that. The line is no shorter than before (that shouldn't matter anyways), and I don't think there is any leaks there. What could cause this? Thanks||||reg||1
Z||000001||pherder||09-10-2000||08:55 PM||||JohnnyK,<P>The original clutch line is still available from GM.  If you look up 14105670 on <A HREF="http://www.gmpartsdirect.com" TARGET=_blank>www.gmpartsdirect.com</A>  you will see that it is approx $56 plus shipping/handling.<P>I did like you tried ... open the bleed screw, push in the slave's pushrod, close the bleed screw, let go the pushrod and then fill the master.  I did that a couple of times and although I got fluid thru the line, it still wasn't bleed.  I then tried Archie's method but still couldn't get all the air out even doing his complete method twice!  Then I just took out the bleed screw and left it.  After a little while, fluid was running out of it so I put back in the bleed screw, filled up the master and tried the clutch and it worked!!!!  I did have the slave below the level of the master so that the fluid would flow downhill AND I had the cap off of the master.  The only thing needed to be looked at was to NOT allow the master to go dry (again ... yup, I drained it all out on the floor the first time.)  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/redface.gif"><P>Paul||||reg||6
Z||000002||Phil||09-10-2000||09:46 PM||||Another way I found to bleed the clutch is to remove the slave from the tranny and point the cylinder down (rod down bleeder up) and bleed like brakes. Then just reinstall the cyl.||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||09-10-2000||09:47 PM||||The whole reason I made my own is so I don't have to pay about 120 canadian to get a stupid steel line.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Anyways, before, it used to work. Push the slave in, and the master would just about drain. I just want to know why it is not pulling all the fluid out now. That doesn't make any sense whatsoever.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> BTW, wouldn't you want the slave higher than the master to bleed? I know with the master high, the fluid would come out, but with the slave, the bubbles would come out?||||reg||1
